{"target":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:a88d4f150edc432c68fe5d9ba7f02daae5c4f28b","text":"Retired because its doorstep is the wrong shape, per hugh 2026-08-10.\n\nI offered a binary — everyone may cause a durable act, or only known accounts may — and both answers assume the connector observes the tracker and then decides what to do with what it found. That does not survive scale. Repositories exist with more than a hundred thousand issues, ordinary projects reach thousands, and under either of my answers the connector reads all of them and the log grows a durable act for a large fraction. It also leaves the attack surface wide: issue content is written by anyone, and my design let all of it in before any filter applied.\n\nThe better shape is admission by clause. The connector observes only what a durable clause admits — either an explicit selection naming particular issues, or standing criteria evaluated each poll. Both are durable acts by an actor who already holds the authority to state them, so neither needs a ratification round-trip: requiring the operator to ratify their own admission clause adds a signature and no information.\n\nThat also gives containment the binary could not. If observations rest on the clause that admitted them, retiring a bad clause flares everything it let in.\n\nReplacement follows."}